  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Data view: Kibana Sample Data Logs** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`)\nThe fields that matter for web traffic are `response` (plus `response.keyword`), `clientip`, `bytes`, `url`, `request`, `host`, `geo.dest`/`geo.src`, `extension`, `machine.os`, `agent` and `referer`.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-73fae8b1 Web logs overview\"**\n- Link: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-73fae8b1-web-logs-overview-ac21b2d30bfe\n- Panels (5):\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n  - Total bytes served (sum of `bytes`)\n  - Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n  - Responses by status code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`, top 10)\n- Time range: last 1 year. I chose this because the sample data's timestamps are relative to when it was installed, and I could not confirm the range.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-73fae8b1 5xx spike\"**\n- Rule ID: `01e55dac-2af3-414b-8aab-19efbb1e36cd`\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (`.es-query`), consumer `stackAlerts`\n- Schedule: every 1 minute\n- Condition: count of documents over the last 5 minutes is greater than 10\n- Query filter: `response.keyword` wildcard `5*`, which matches any 5xx status\n- Enabled, so it is running now. Its status is currently `ok`.\n\nI could not check the actual response-code distribution or confirm the rule fires. The Elasticsearch endpoint is not reachable from here (`localhost:9200` refused connections), and the Kibana ES|QL and console proxies returned 404 and 400. The filter is written from the field metadata, not from a tested query.",
